diff --git a/native/cpu/cpu_native.c b/native/cpu/cpu_native.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f06cac51cf9a6e6849fa33bb85abacd706a2ee8f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/native/cpu/cpu_native.c
@@ -0,0 +1,2220 @@
+/*
+ * hakuX native core — the guest CPU on an x86-64 host: the title's code runs
+ * natively in 32-bit compatibility mode (cpu.h, cpu_native.h).
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 Press5elect
+ * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
+ * Additional terms (GPL-3.0 section 7(b)): keep the attribution "OGBox by
+ * Press5elect" (github.com/Press5elect/OGBox, NOTICE).
+ *
+ * The mechanisms are Kelvin's (PS5_Kelvin src/core: cpu.c, fault.c,
+ * xpatch.c; same author and licence), fitted to hakuX's kernel, which wants
+ * FEX's shape: one vCPU host thread, system ops handed to a callback with
+ * the guest's frame, guest threads switched by rewriting that frame.
+ *
+ * Entering: ncn_enter loads the frame and iretq's into the compat code
+ * segment. Leaving: every guest exit is a fault on the vCPU thread, handled
+ * on its alternate signal stack with the interrupted ucontext as the frame:
+ *  - #GP (SIGSEGV, trap 13): int n (the kernel's int 0x3F), cli/sti, hlt,
+ *    in/out, the ring-0 instructions, and fs: accesses (Linux's null FS
+ *    selector: the 0x64 prefix becomes 0x65, GS being the KPCR, and the
+ *    instruction runs again);
+ *  - #UD (SIGILL): the code sweep's ud2 for rdtsc, cpuid, sidt, sgdt;
+ *  - #BP (SIGTRAP): its int3 for pushfd/popfd, the virtual IF's;
+ *  - #PF: the core's (device registers, write tracking, paging, the title's
+ *    #PF handler: nc_core_fault, through the handler installed before ours).
+ * The ucontext's registers, flags and FXSAVE image become the frame, the
+ * system op runs (it may switch threads: the frame is another's after it)
+ * and the frame goes back into the ucontext, which the signal's return
+ * resumes. HLT ends nc_cpu_run (siglongjmp), as FEX's exit on HLT.
+ *
+ * Kernel calls through the gate (phase 2): a far call from the kernel's
+ * trampolines into 64-bit code (cpu_native_asm.S) saves the frame and runs
+ * the same system op without a signal.
+ *
+ * Interrupts: nc_cpu_request_interrupt sets the request and signals the
+ * vCPU thread (NC_KICK_SIGNAL). Its handler, in guest code, runs
+ * NC_SYSOP_INTERRUPT as FEX does at a block start (the kernel switches to
+ * its ISR/DPC threads); in host code it leaves the request to the system
+ * op's end, which takes it on the way back. Inside the kernel's fast paths
+ * (guest code that keeps the state of a call half made) none is taken:
+ * the guest is single-stepped out first.
+ *
+ * IF: the guest's is virtual (ring 3 can't clear the real one): cli/sti
+ * trap, pushfd/popfd are the sweep's int3s, and the title's #PF handler
+ * returns with an iretd that faults (its frame's CS is 0, kernel.c) and is
+ * completed here with the IF it pops.
+ *
+ * RSQRT sites (NC_RSQRT_SITES, opt-in, off by default): Intel's estimates for
+ * listed RSQRTSS/RSQRTPS instructions on an AMD host, the sites' int3s
+ * trapping (see "RSQRT sites" below).
+ */
+#include "native/cpu/cpu_native.h"
+#include "native/cpu/rsqrt.h"
+#include "native/cpu/x86dec.h"
+#include "native/cpu/x86emu.h"
+#include "native/cpu/vmhost.h"
+
